From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] aic8800: let cfg80211 govern this radio, instead of claiming self-management with no domain

Known issue: `phy0` is `REGULATORY_WIPHY_SELF_MANAGED`, so the regulatory
database this image now loads correctly (`CONFIG_CRYPTO_SHA256=y`
plus the upstream `regulatory.db`) governs nothing on this radio. Measured on the
board 2026-08-15: `iw reg get` reports `phy#0 (self-managed)`, `country 00:
DFS-UNSET`, `(2380 - 2520 @ 40)`, `(5140 - 5980 @ 80)` -- two rules covering
channels 1-14 and 5150-5925 with no DFS, no indoor-only and no per-band power
limit, against a global domain that marks 5250-5330 and 5490-5730 DFS.

What the vendor driver does and what this patch changes, read in the pinned source
(`src/USB/driver_fw/drivers/aic8800/aic8800_fdrv/rwnx_mod_params.c`, tag
`5.0+git20260123.5f7be68d-8`):

| the vendor | ours |
|---|---|
| `custregd` compiled in as `true` (line 59, under a vendor comment new in 5.0 at line 58 that names both modes) | `false` -- the permissive path is opt-in |
| `MODULE_PARM_DESC` says `(Default: 0)` for that same parameter (line 170) | the code now matches the text the vendor already wrote |
| on the previous pin, `rwnx_custregd()` set `REGULATORY_WIPHY_SELF_MANAGED` **before** the `custregd` early return, so a build with the parameter off still declared self-management and then applied no domain at all | fixed upstream in 5.0 -- the return now comes first (lines 1845-1846) and the flag write follows it (line 1849) -- so this patch no longer moves it |
| `REGULATORY_IGNORE_STALE_KICKOFF` still written unconditionally, in 5.0 just before that return (line 1843) | dropped: the symbol does not exist in the kernel we build against, and the driver's own `rwnx_compat.h:244,249` defines it to `0` for exactly that case, so the write ORed a zero and only read as if it did something |
| the parameter's help text does not say what it costs | it names the consequence: the loaded `regulatory.db` governs nothing on this radio |

Verified in our tree, not assumed:

- `REGULATORY_IGNORE_STALE_KICKOFF` appears nowhere in `linux-6.18.36`
  (`grep -rn` over the whole tree: no hit). The only definitions are the driver's
  own compat header.
- The BSP ships the same driver with the return already before the flags and
  `custregd` defaulting to `false`
  (`drivers/net/wireless/aic8800/aic8800_fdrv/rwnx_mod_params.c`, as found in the
  vendor BSP). We do not copy its version guard
  `#if LINUX_VERSION_CODE < KERNEL_VERSION(6, 4, 0)` around the stale-kickoff
  write: we have not verified which release removed the symbol, and we do not
  need to -- this module is built against one kernel, where the symbol is absent
  and the compat header makes the write a no-op.
- The firmware power-limit table is selected from the same `country_code[]`
  buffer that the `custregd` path fills, which would make an empty country a real
  regression -- except that `CONFIG_POWER_LIMIT = n` in the driver's own
  `aic8800_fdrv/Makefile:118`, so `rwnx_plat_powerlimit_load_8800d80()` is not
  compiled into what we ship. Recorded because it is the trap this change would
  otherwise walk into.

What this does NOT do, stated rather than left to be discovered:

- ⚠ **In 5.0 the notifier changed, and with this patch its new branch runs.**
  `wiphy->reg_notifier = rwnx_reg_notifier` (`rwnx_main.c:9059`). On the previous
  pin the body only set the DFS region and forwarded the country to the firmware
  for AIC8801, 8800DC, 8800DW, 8800D81, 8800D81X2 and 8800D89X2 -- not our D80.
  In 5.0, whenever `custregd` is off (`rwnx_main.c:6503-6522`), it first calls
  `regulatory_hint(wiphy, alpha2)` from inside the notifier, guarded only by a
  100 ms "suspicious rapid reg change" check against the last alpha2, and then
  `rwnx_send_me_chan_config_req()` for **every** part when `testmode == 0`. So
  with this patch the D80's firmware does now receive the country, and cfg80211
  gets a driver hint from this wiphy on each regulatory change. Neither was
  exercised by the proof below.
- ⓘ What the patch does buy that path: a self-managed wiphy takes its domain from
  the driver, so the notifier only ever saw what the driver itself set. With the
  flag gone, the DFS region it applies is the one cfg80211 resolved.
- It sets no country. Out of the box the radio falls to the world domain until
  something hints one (`iw reg set DE`, or `country=` in the supplicant config).
  That is an image-side decision and is deliberately not smuggled into a driver
  patch.
- 5.0 refuses the driver's private country-set commands while `custregd` is off:
  `aic_vendor.c:328`, `aic_priv_cmd.c:1392` and `:2059` log "invalid custregd"
  and return -EINVAL. With this patch the country comes from cfg80211 only.

⬜ On the previous pin this patch is HW-proven (2026-09-09: no
`self-managed` line, and DE / US / JP resolve to DFS-ETSI / DFS-FCC / DFS-JP).
That proof does NOT carry over to 5.0, because every hint now runs through the
notifier's new branch. The falsifier on 5.0 is one board session: `iw reg get`
must still not say `self-managed` for `phy#0`; `iw reg set DE` must still produce
`country DE: DFS-ETSI` with the 5250-5330 / 5490-5730 blocks marked DFS; and with
`aicwf_dbg_level` raised to 0xb, one `iw reg set` must not make the notifier
re-enter itself (repeated "regulatory domain set to" lines, or "suspicious rapid
reg change").

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/src/USB/driver_fw/drivers/aic8800/aic8800_fdrv/rwnx_mod_params.c b/src/USB/driver_fw/drivers/aic8800/aic8800_fdrv/rwnx_mod_params.c
index d386706..f0ed4fb 100644
--- a/src/USB/driver_fw/drivers/aic8800/aic8800_fdrv/rwnx_mod_params.c
+++ b/src/USB/driver_fw/drivers/aic8800/aic8800_fdrv/rwnx_mod_params.c
@@ -56,7 +56,7 @@ struct rwnx_mod_params rwnx_mod_params = {
     COMMON_PARAM(mutx_on, true, true)
     COMMON_PARAM(use_80, true, true)
 /* false: use crda(iw reg set CN); true: drive self-management(wifi_test wlan0 country_set CN) */
-    COMMON_PARAM(custregd, true, true)
+    COMMON_PARAM(custregd, false, false)
     COMMON_PARAM(custchan, false, false)
     COMMON_PARAM(roc_dur_max, 500, 500)
     COMMON_PARAM(listen_itv, 0, 0)
@@ -168,7 +168,10 @@ MODULE_PARM_DESC(use_80, "Enable 80MHz (Default: 1)");
 
 module_param_named(custregd, rwnx_mod_params.custregd, bool, S_IRUGO);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(custregd,
-                 "Use permissive custom regulatory rules (for testing ONLY) (Default: 0)");
+                 "Use permissive custom regulatory rules (for testing ONLY). "
+                 "Takes the wiphy out of cfg80211's regulatory control - the "
+                 "loaded regulatory.db then governs nothing on this radio. "
+                 "(Default: 0)");
 
 module_param_named(custchan, rwnx_mod_params.custchan, bool, S_IRUGO);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(custchan,
@@ -1839,9 +1842,11 @@ void rwnx_custregd(struct rwnx_hw *rwnx_hw, struct wiphy *wiphy)
 // For older kernel version, the custom regulatory is applied before the wiphy
 // registration (in rwnx_set_wiphy_params()), so nothing has to be done here
 
-#if LINUX_VERSION_CODE >= KERNEL_VERSION(4, 0, 0)
-	wiphy->regulatory_flags |= REGULATORY_IGNORE_STALE_KICKOFF;
-#endif
+	/* REGULATORY_IGNORE_STALE_KICKOFF is not written here. The symbol no longer
+	 * exists in the kernel this module is built against, and rwnx_compat.h
+	 * defines it to 0 for exactly that case, so the assignment ORed a zero and
+	 * only read as though it did something.
+	 */
 	if (!rwnx_hw->mod_params->custregd)
 		return;
 
